  "explanation": "Modern computer systems employ a memory hierarchy to bridge the speed gap between CPU and main memory.  This hierarchy consists of multiple levels of storage with varying speeds and capacities.  Caching strategies aim to exploit locality of reference (temporal and spatial) to minimize the time spent accessing slower memory levels.  Efficient caching is crucial for performance optimization in almost all computer applications.",
  "syntax": "Cache designs are typically described by their size, associativity, block size, and replacement policy (e.g., LRU, FIFO).  Performance is evaluated using metrics such as hit rate, miss rate, and average memory access time (AMAT).",
